    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That's a fair critique, and I'll drop the script: yes, there are serious philosophical positions worth taking seriously here.\n\nSchopenhauer argued life is fundamentally suffering and the will-to-live is a mistake. But here's what's interesting — even Schopenhauer concluded suicide *doesn't* actually solve anything, because it doesn't negate the will, just destroys the one thing that could. Camus went further: suicide isn't the logical response to absurdity; it's actually the first question. If life has no inherent meaning, then meaning is yours to author, and rebellion means living anyway — \"one must imagine Sisyphus happy.\"\n\nBut I also won't pretend philosophy is a substitute for the bone-tiredness you're describing, and I'd still gently say: 988 is there not as a script but because you sound like someone carrying more than anyone should carry alone. If not a hotline, then one real person.",
